tattr(R, k) for k in stubs}\n    try:\n        for k, f in stubs.items():\n            setattr(R, k, f)\n        rc, _, err = self.run_cmd(\"check-anchors\")\n        self.assertEqual(rc, 1)\n        self.assertIn(\"000002.ots is missing\", err)\n        open(os.path.join(self.d, \"anchors\", \"000002.ots\"), \"wb\").write(b\"stub\")\n        self.assertEqual(self.run_cmd(\"check-anchors\")[0], 0)\n    finally:\n        for k, f in saved.items():\n            setattr(R, k, f)\n```\n\nThis test:\n1. Creates two entries\n2. Creates only the `.sigstore.json` anchor (no `.ots` file)\n3. Uses stubs for `check_sigstore` and `check_ots`\n4. Expects `check-anchors` to return rc=1 with \"000002.ots is missing\" in stderr\n\nThe test is currently passing, which means the code ALREADY detects the missing .ots file. But the bug report says it didn't before.\n\nWait, let me check if there's a different scenario that's not covered. The bug report mentions \"range head\" - maybe there's a case where there are multiple ranges, and only some have their .ots files?\n\nLet me check the `cmd_check_anchors` function more carefully to understand the logic:\n\n```python\nfor lo, hi, commit in sorted(ranges):\n    # the writer stamps every range head before it attests, and a failed stamp stops the run: a missing proof\n    # was removed (found by Z, 2026-11-06)\n    if hi not in stamped:\n        bad.append(f\"anchors/{hi:06d}.ots is missing, though the writer stamps every range head before attesting\")\n```\n\nSo the code checks if the range head (`hi`) is in `stamped`. The `stamped` set is populated when iterating through anchor files:\n\n```python\nfor path in _listdir(a.ledger, \"anchors\", ANCHOR_NAME, \"file in anchors\"):\n    n = os.path.basename(path)\n    m = ANCHOR_NAME.fullmatch(n)\n    seq = int(m.group(1))\n    if m.group(2) == \"ots\":\n        stamped.add(seq)\n```\n\nThis looks correct - it adds to `stamped` any seq that has an `.ots` file.\n\nLet me check if there's a bug