{"id": "de386a0d-6846-4aec-b235-82ea7e193df9", "timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:50:09.742219", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call-ffe5d324-2890-4c9e-9d5f-8d11cc77509a", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/record/record.py:\n   383\tdef cmd_check_anchors(a):\n   384\t    \"\"\"Every anchor must be right, the writer's Sigstore ranges must cover entries 1..head with no gap or overlap,\n   385\t    and every range head must have its OpenTimestamps proof.\n   386\t    Checks every anchor even after a failure; exit 1 if anything was wrong, else 2 if anything couldn't be checked.\"\"\"\n   387\t    chain, _ = load_chain(a.ledger)\n   388\t    rows, bad, cant, ranges, stamped = [], [], [], [], set()\n   389\t    pending, attested_at = [], {}\n   390\t    for path in _listdir(a.ledger, \"anchors\", ANCHOR_NAME, \"file in anchors\"):\n   391\t        n = os.path.basename(path)\n   392\t        m = ANCHOR_NAME.fullmatch(n)\n   393\t        seq = int(m.group(1))\n   394\t        if m.group(2) == \"ots\":\n   395\t            stamped.add(seq)\n   396\t        try:\n   397\t            if not 1 <= seq <= len(chain):\n   398\t                raise Bad(f\"anchors/{n}: no entry {seq}\")\n   399\t            raw = open(os.path.join(a.ledger, \"entries\", f\"{seq:06d}.json\"), \"rb\").read()\n   400\t            if m.group(2) == \"ots\":\n   401\t                t = check_ots(path, raw, a.explorer)\n   402\t                rows.append({\"anchor\": n, \"kind\": \"opentimestamps\", \"existed_by\": iso(t) or \"pending\"})\n   403\t                if t is None:\n   404\t                    pending.append(seq)\n   405\t            else:\n   406\t                t, pred, commit = check_sigstore(path, chain[seq - 1][1], a.repo, a.workflow, a.ref)\n   407\t                if not isinstance(pred, dict) or not isinstance(pred.get(\"from_seq\"), int) or pred.get(\"to_seq\") != seq \\\n   408\t                        or not 1 <= pred[\"from_seq\"] <= seq or pred != range_predicate(chain, pred[\"from_seq\"], seq):\n   409\t                    raise Bad(f\"anchors/{n}: attested range {pred!r} doesn't match this chain\")\n   410\t                ranges.append((pred[\"from_seq\"], seq, commit))\n   411\t                attested_at[seq] = t\n   412\t                rows.append({\"anchor\": n, \"kind\": \"sigstore\", \"entries\": f\"{pred['from_seq']}-{seq}\", \"existed_by\": iso(t),\n   413\t                             \"writer_commit\": commit})\n   414\t        except Bad as ex:\n   415\t            bad.append(str(ex))\n   416\t        except CantCheck as ex:\n   417\t            cant.append(f\"anchors/{n}: {ex}\")\n   418\t    for lo, hi, commit in sorted(ranges):\n   419\t        # the writer stamps every range head before it attests, and a failed stamp stops the run: a missing proof\n   420\t        # was removed (found by Z, 2026-11-06)\n   421\t        if hi not in stamped:\n   422\t            bad.append(f\"anchors/{hi:06d}.ots is missing, though the writer stamps every range head before attesting\")\n   423\t    if a.max_pending_hours is not None:\n   424\t        # a proof still pending long after its Sigstore time means the daily upgrade isn't running (it failed silently\n   425\t        # for its first five days; review 2026-11-11). Opt-in, so the writer's own pre-check never blocks on it.\n   426\t        for seq in pending:\n   427\t            t = attested_at.get(seq)\n   428\t            if t is not None and time.time() - t > a.max_pending_hours * 3600:\n   429\t                bad.append(f\"anchors/{seq:06d}.ots is still pending {(time.time() - t) / 3600:.0f} h after its attestation \"\n   430\t                           f\"(limit {a.max_pending_hours} h): is the daily upgrade running?\")\n   431\t    covered, changes, prev_commit = 0, [], None\n   432\t    for lo, hi, commit in sorted(ranges):\n   433\t        if lo != covered + 1:\n   434\t            bad.append(f\"the writer's ranges {'overlap' if lo <= covered else 'leave a gap'} at entry {min(lo, covered + 1)}\")\n   435\t        covered = max(covered, hi)\n   436\t        if prev_commit:  # did the writer's code change between these two runs? (information: a stranger reads the diff)\n   437\t            changes.append({\"entries\": f\"{lo}-{hi}\", \"from_commit\": prev_commit, \"to_commit\": commit,\n   438\t                            \"writer_code_changed\": _code_changed(a, prev_commit, commit)})\n   439\t        prev_commit = commit\n   440\t    if covered < len(chain):\n   441\t        bad.append(f\"entries {covered + 1}-{len(chain)} were never attested by the writer\")\n   442\t    print(json.dumps({\"anchors\": rows, \"entries\": len(chain), \"writer_attested_through\": covered,\n   443\t                      \"writer_runs\": changes, \"wrong\": bad, \"could_not_check\": cant}, indent=1))\n   444\t    for x in bad:\n   445\t        print(f\"BAD: {x}\", file=sys.stderr)\n   446\t    for x in cant:\n   447\t        print(f\"CAN'T CHECK: {x}\", file=sys.stderr)\n   448\t    return 1 if bad else 2 if cant else 0\n"}], "is_error": false, "command": "view", "path": "/home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/record/record.py", "prev_exist": true, "old_content": null, "new_content": null, "kind": "FileEditorObservation"}, "action_id": "0e3dc6de-7404-4303-a2d9-be69d3484a2d", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "b5b2ca1a-7a9e-4080-8f3a-85b4ae4b5fbd", "timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:51:01.513811", "source": "agent", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Now I understand the issue. Let me trace through the logic:\n\n1. The loop at line 390 iterates over ALL anchor files\n2. For `.ots` files, it adds the sequence to `stamped` set (line 395)\n3. For `.sigstore.json` files, it tries to verify them (line 406) and ONLY if successful, adds to `ranges` (line 410)\n4. After the loop, it checks each range in `ranges` to see if its head (`hi`) is in `stamped` (line 421)\n\n**The bug**: If `check_sigstore` raises `CantCheck` (e.g., sigstore-python not installed), the range is never added to `ranges`, so the `.ots` check never runs for that range head.\n\nLet me verify this is the issue by creating a test:\n"}], "reasoning_content": "Now I understand the issue. The problem is in the logic. The code iterates through ALL anchor files in the anchors directory (both `.sigstore.json` and `.ots` files). When it finds a `.sigstore.json` file, it processes it and adds the range to the `ranges` list. When it finds a `.ots` file, it adds the sequence to the `stamped` set.\n\nBut here's the issue: the check at lines 418-422 only runs for the ranges found in the `ranges` list (which only includes ranges from `.sigstore.json` files that were successfully verified). If the `.sigstore.json` file is found but the `.ots` file is missing, the check at line 421-422 should catch it.\n\nLet me trace through the logic:\n1. The loop at line 390 iterates over ALL anchor files (both `.ots` and `.sigstore.json`)\n2. When it finds a `.ots` file, it adds the sequence to `stamped` (line 395)\n3. When it finds a `.sigstore.json` file, it tries to verify it (line 406) and if successful, adds the range to `ranges` (line 410)\n3. After the loop, for each range in `ranges`, it checks if the range head (`hi`) is in `stamped` (line 421)\n\nSo the bug is that if the `.sigstore.json` file can't be checked (e.g., sigstore-python is not installed), it raises `CantCheck` and the range is never added to `ranges`. Then the check at line 421 never runs for that range head.\n\nLet me verify this theory by looking at the test case again:\n\n```python\ndef test_a_missing_ots_proof_for_a_range_head_is_caught(self):\n    # Z, 2026-11-06: deleting a range head's .ots left every check at exit 0. Anchor checks are stubbed here\n    # (CI checks real ones); what's tested is that check-anchors notices the proof is gone.\n    self._two_entries()\n    chain, _ = R.load_chain(self.d)\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(self.d, \"anchors\"))\n    open(os.path.join(self.d, \"anchors\", \"000002.sigstore.json\"), \"w\").write(\"{}\")\n    stubs = {\"check_sigstore\": lambda *a: (1, R.range_predicate(chain, 1, 2), \"c0ffee\"), \"check_ots\": lambda *a: None}\n    saved = {k: getattr(R, k) for k in stubs}\n    try:\n        for k, f in stubs.items():\n            setattr(R, k, f)\n        rc, _, err = self.run_cmd(\"check-anchors\")\n        self.assertEqual(rc, 1)\n        self.assertIn(\"000002.ots is missing\", err)\n        open(os.path.join(self.d, \"anchors\", \"000002.ots\"), \"wb\").write(b\"stub\")\n        self.assertEqual(self.run_cmd(\"check-anchors\")[0], 0)\n    finally:\n        for k, f in saved.items():\n            setattr(R, k, f)\n```\n\nThe test passes because the stubs are provided and `check_sigstore` returns successfully. The bug occurs when `check_sigstore` raises `CantCheck` (e.g., when sigstore-python is not installed), so the range never gets added to `ranges` and the missing `.ots` check never runs.\n\nThe fix should be to collect all range heads from `.sigstore.json` files (whether they can be verified or not) and check if their corresponding `.ots` files exist. Let me think about how to implement this.\n\nActually, the current logic is:\n1. Iterate all anchor files\n2. For `.sigstore.json` files, try to verify them and if successful, add to `ranges`\n3. After processing all files, check if each range head in `ranges` has a corresponding `.ots` file in `stamped`\n\nThe bug is that if verification fails (CantCheck), the range is not added to `ranges`, so the `.ots` check is skipped.\n\nThe fix could be:\n- Instead of only adding to `ranges` on successful verification, we should also track range heads from `.sigstore.json` files that couldn't be verified (but exist)\n- Or, we should check for the existence of `.ots` files for all `.sigstore.json` files regardless of verification status\n\nLet me think about what the correct behavior should be:\n- If a `.sigstore.json` file exists for a range head, the corresponding `.ots` file MUST exist\n- The check for `.ots` should happen regardless of whether we can verify the `.sigstore.json` file\n\nSo the fix should be to:\n1. First pass: collect all `.sigstore.json` files and their sequence numbers (range heads)\n2. Second pass: collect all `.ots` files and their sequence numbers\n3. Check that every range head from `.sigstore.json` has a corresponding `.ots` file\n\nBut wait, the current code already iterates all files. The problem is the conditional logic:\n- If it's an `.ots` file, add to `stamped`\n- If it's a `.sigstore.json` file, try to verify and if successful, add to `ranges`\n\nThe fix should be to track range heads from `.sigstore.json` files separately from whether they were verified. Let me implement this.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the code:\n- Line 390: `for path in _listdir(a.ledger, \"anchors\", ANCHOR_NAME, \"file in anchors\"):`\n- Line 393-395: For `.ots` files, add seq to `stamped`\n- Line 406-413: For `.sigstore.json` files, try to verify and if successful, add to `ranges` and `attested_at`\n- Line 418-422: Check each range in `ranges` to see if its head is in `stamped`\n\nThe issue is that if line 406 raises `CantCheck`, the range is never added to `ranges`, so line 421 never checks it.\n\nThe fix: We need to track the range heads from `.sigstore.json` files even if verification fails. We could:\n1. Add the range head to a set (e.g., `sigstore_heads`) before attempting verification\n2. After the loop, check that all heads in `sigstore_heads` are in `stamped`\n\nLet me implement this fix.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cd /home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/record && python3 -c \"\nimport os, tempfile, json, shutil, hashlib\nimport record as R\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\n# Create a test ledger\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\n\n# Create two entries\nPLAN = {'by': 'proposer-agent', 'claim': 'mlp beats linear', 'test': 'count.py ...', 'decision_rule': 'Ir lower by > 2 SE'}\n\n# Entry 1\ne1 = {'seq': 1, 'prev': None, 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': datetime.now().isoformat(), 'body': PLAN}\nwith open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'wb') as f:\n    f.write(R.canonical(e1))\n\n# Entry 2\ne2 = {'seq': 2, 'prev': hashlib.sha256(open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'rb').read()).hexdigest(), 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': datetime.now().isoformat(), 'body': dict(PLAN, claim='second')}\nwith open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000002.json'), 'wb') as f:\n    f.write(R.canonical(e2))\n\n# Create sigstore anchor for range 1-2 (head is 2)\nwith open(os.path.join(d, 'anchors', '000002.sigstore.json'), 'w') as f:\n    f.write('{}')\n\n# Run check-anchors with check_sigstore raising CantCheck\ndef bad_check_sigstore(*a):\n    raise R.CantCheck('sigstore-python not installed')\n\nstubs = {'check_sigstore': bad_check_sigstore, 'check_ots': lambda *a: None}\nsaved = {k: getattr(R, k) for k in stubs}\ntry:\n    for k, f in stubs.items():\n        setattr(R, k, f)\n    \n    import sys, io, contextlib\n    out, err = io.StringIO(), io.StringIO()\n    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):\n        rc = R.main(['--ledger', d, 'check-anchors'])\n    print(f'RC: {rc}')\n    print(f'STDOUT: {out.getvalue()}')\n    print(f'STDERR: {err.getvalue()}')\nfinally:\n    for k, f in saved.items():\n        setattr(R, k, f)\n\nshutil.rmtree(d)\n\"", "is_input": false, "timeout": null, "reset": false, "kind": "TerminalAction"}, "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "call-7b52cd72-2951-4727-ac71-8fcb39643082", "tool_call": {"id": "call-7b52cd72-2951-4727-ac71-8fcb39643082", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "terminal", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/record && python3 -c \\\"\\nimport os, tempfile, json, shutil, hashlib\\nimport record as R\\nfrom datetime import datetime\\n\\n# Create a test ledger\\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\\n\\n# Create two entries\\nPLAN = {'by': 'proposer-agent', 'claim': 'mlp beats linear', 'test': 'count.py ...', 'decision_rule': 'Ir lower by > 2 SE'}\\n\\n# Entry 1\\ne1 = {'seq': 1, 'prev': None, 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': datetime.now().isoformat(), 'body': PLAN}\\nwith open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'wb') as f:\\n    f.write(R.canonical(e1))\\n\\n# Entry 2\\ne2 = {'seq': 2, 'prev': hashlib.sha256(open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'rb').read()).hexdigest(), 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': datetime.now().isoformat(), 'body': dict(PLAN, claim='second')}\\nwith open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000002.json'), 'wb') as f:\\n    f.write(R.canonical(e2))\\n\\n# Create sigstore anchor for range 1-2 (head is 2)\\nwith open(os.path.join(d, 'anchors', '000002.sigstore.json'), 'w') as f:\\n    f.write('{}')\\n\\n# Run check-anchors with check_sigstore raising CantCheck\\ndef bad_check_sigstore(*a):\\n    raise R.CantCheck('sigstore-python not installed')\\n\\nstubs = {'check_sigstore': bad_check_sigstore, 'check_ots': lambda *a: None}\\nsaved = {k: getattr(R, k) for k in stubs}\\ntry:\\n    for k, f in stubs.items():\\n        setattr(R, k, f)\\n    \\n    